1. Need and Benefits of Component LifecycleIn React, components go through a series of steps from creation to removal. Managing these stages allows you to execute code at specific points in a component's existence.Need: Real-world applications require tasks like fetching external API data, setting up timers, manually manipulating the DOM, or cleaning up resources when elements leave the screen.Benefits:Resource Management: Prevents memory leaks by cleaning up listeners or subscriptions.Performance Optimization: Controls when a component re-renders to avoid unnecessary computations.Predictable Behavior: Provides standard entry points for data fetching and error handling.2. Three Main Phases of the Component LifecycleThe React Class Component lifecycle consists of three primary phases:Mounting: When the component is created and inserted into the DOM.Updating: When the component re-renders due to changes in props or state.Unmounting: When the component is removed from the DOM.Key Lifecycle Hook MethodsPhaseHook MethodPurposeMountingconstructor()Initializes state and binds event handler methods.Mountingrender()Returns the JSX element tree to be painted on screen.MountingcomponentDidMount()Triggered immediately after component mounts. Ideal for API calls or subscriptions.UpdatingcomponentDidUpdate()Triggered immediately after updating occurs. Useful for DOM operations after state updates.UnmountingcomponentWillUnmount()Executed right before a component is unmounted and destroyed. Used for cleanup tasks.Error HandlingcomponentDidCatch()Catches JavaScript errors anywhere in the child component tree and logs or handles them.3. Sequence of Steps in Rendering a ComponentWhen a class component renders for the first time, React executes methods in this order:constructor(): Sets initial state and binds handler methods.static getDerivedStateFromProps(): Syncs state with props if necessary (rarely used).render(): Evaluates JSX and returns the UI structure.React updates DOM: Nodes are inserted into the browser tree.componentDidMount(): Invoked once DOM rendering is complete. Perfect for side effects (e.g., Fetch API calls).